course: dictionary
lesson: 8068
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Release is one of them. To find the right meaning, read the words around it. «The zoo will release the young frogs into the mountains.» Here release means to set free, because the frogs are let go into the wild.
explain: «The game company will release its new game next month.» Here release means to make something available to the public, so people can buy and play it. Ask yourself: is something being set free, or is something being made public? The clues tell you which.
explain: «Burning wood releases heat and light.» Here release means to let something come out. Heat and light escape into the air. To check your answer, swap in a meaning and read the sentence again. If it still makes sense, you have probably found the right one.
fact: Release means to let go or set free, as in «release a bird».
fact: Release also means to make a film, song, game or book available to the public.
fact: Release can mean to let something like gas, energy or heat come out, as in «plants release oxygen».
fact: «A release» is the act of letting go, or a new film, game or song. «A new release» is something that has just come out.
fact: Phrases with release include «press release» and «release the brake».
exercise: «The prisoners were released after ten years.» What does release mean here? | *To set free | To make available to the public | To cook slowly | To let heat come out
exercise: «Burning them releases energy but also smoke.» Which meaning fits? | To set an animal free | To make a game available | *To let something come out | To stop moving
exercise: «The new film will be a release next Friday.» What is a release here? | A place where prisoners live | A rope used for climbing | *A new film made available to the public | A quiet, gentle sound
test: «Plants release oxygen into the air.» What does release mean? | To stop breathing | *To let something come out | To set free a dog | To make a film
test: «Remember to release the brake before you start.» What does release the brake mean? | To press the brake harder | To take the brake off the shelf | *To let go of the brake so the car can move | To paint the brake
exam: Ella says release only means to set free. Is she right? | *No, release can also mean to make a game or film available to the public | Yes, release only ever means to set free | Yes, release is the same as a brake | No, release means to cook food slowly
